JM Levine wrote operation Gemini  it's inspired by true events and the  script Chronicles the struggle of a  desperate mother to rescue her newborn  twins from the chaos of the war in the  Ukraine please join me in welcoming JM  Jonathan to the stage  [Applause]  uh hello everybody  um  you know I'd like to say thank you to  the academy and to the members of the  nickel committee I'm humbled and shocked  by all of this  I'd also like to say what a pleasure has  been getting to know the other fellows  this year present and past and uh  sharing in their belief that this has  all been a big mistake  um my script operation Jim and I  should not exist as many of you probably  gathered from its topicality it was  written with great speed  on March 8th I was driving to pick up my  daughter from daycare the nickel late  deadline this year was May 2nd and a  story came on the radio and Kiev right  as Russia started its invasion of  Ukraine a woman gave birth to twins the  one was a surrogate for a couple in  Chicago and the couple contacted project  Dynamo to get the woman and her babies  out of the country project Dynamo picked  them up drove them 13 hours through ice  storms and checkpoints to meet the  family from Chicago on the border of  Poland  I heard this and thought this sounds  like a pretty cool movie I mean saving  babies in a war zone come on  um it felt simple it felt propulsive  felt like it would be a good read  um but I didn't really have the time or  the energy I have a four-year-old  daughter demanding day job as a  executive or production company and to  be honest  writing it felt like a waste of time  it's been over a decade writing spec  feature after respect feature after  respect feature after TV pilot after TV  pod after pitch deck after spec feature  and taking meeting after meeting after  meeting  and I just really couldn't bring myself  to do it again I was still developing  and creating things for my job and that  was fine but there was no real reason to  write another feature  um but still there was something about  this story and he kept gnawing at me so  I told my wife Marion about it I said  hey so I heard this story about this  woman who gave birth to twins during the  Russian invasion this team went into and  she said stop stop  it's too much I can't handle babies in a  war zone right now  a very uncharacteristic response from  her is this an emotional reaction just a  sentence  as a husband I felt bad didn't mean to  stress her out but as a writer my  reaction was oh  maybe there's something here  in fact I was convinced that I'd go  online a year from now and see this was  being made into a movie and I'd have the  reaction that I'm sure plenty of you in  this room have had  where you'd curse and say I had it first  writer shouts and clouds  so I went for it I spent the next seven  weeks working on working for an hour a  night maybe two if things were going  really well doing my best not to ruin a  simple idea  I finished a draft my wife was the only  person who read it gave me notes back  less than 12 hours and then I submitted  it  but one of the most striking notes  Marion gave me when she read it was you  know you are your main character in this  script you are Sam  well in the script Sam was an  electrician from New Jersey who goes to  Ukraine because he just felt a calling  to be there a calling to help and he's  an amalgamation of real selfless people  that I read about who just flocked to  the country to try to make a difference  but Sam is broken  he is there certainly to help but also  because he has nowhere else to be  he has no family he has lost himself and  doesn't see a Way Forward  running head first into a war zone and  saving these children is his way of  finding his worth and rediscovering  himself  so Marion pointed this out I had a  little revelation  I had to write this story I needed to  write this story  I had to find myself again just as Sam  had to save these twins I had to 